WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.160
Default credentials and sample accounts remain dangerous because attackers know where to

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:06.340
look for them.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.680
Any default identity, password, token,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.900
or administrative endpoint should be removed or changed before production use.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.760
Open cloud storage, public management interfaces, permissive network rules,

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.180
and exposed dashboards can turn a configuration oversight into direct data exposure or

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.160
administrative compromise.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.940
Unnecessary services increase attack surface.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.160
Every enabled port, plugin, feature flag, debugging endpoint,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.460
and administrative function should have a business reason and a documented owner.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.040
Debug mode exposure can leak stack traces, environment variables, credentials, paths,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.100
and framework internals.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.240
Detailed diagnostics belong in protected logs, not public responses.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.580
Use secure baselines for servers, containers, cloud services, and applications.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.240
Apply hardening standards and verify them continuously.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.620
Manage configuration through repeatable, reviewed, and monitored processes.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.960
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include CM-2 - Baseline Configuration;

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.060
CM-6 - Configuration Settings; CM-7 - Least Functionality.
